Search for dental marketing ideas and you get lists of fifty things: start a podcast, sponsor a little league team, post on three social networks every day. Most practices read the list, try two of the easier ones, and go back to work.

This is a shorter list. It is the set of dental marketing ideas we have seen bring patients through the door, in the order we would do them in a practice that is not yet full. One of our clients, a dental practice in Dallas, is the example running through it, because they did most of these in six months and the result is on our [results page](/results/).

## 1. Write to the patients you already have

Before you pay for a stranger's attention, look at the people who already trust you. Every practice has years of patients in its software who finished a course of treatment, or simply stopped coming for cleanings, and were never invited back.

Write to them in the dentist's own voice. Not a newsletter, not a template with a logo. A short note that says it has been a while, the practice would like to see them, and here is the easy way to book. Then ask for a review after every one of those visits goes well.

In Dallas, that recall campaign went to 412 patients who had not been in for a long time. It was one part of what filled the schedule, and it is the part that costs nothing in ads.

## 2. Fix what happens after 5 pm

This is the idea most lists leave out, and it is the one that decides whether the rest of the list works.

When we started with the Dallas practice, their marketing looked fine from the outside. An ad agency was reporting clicks. The website looked good. But two hygienists were idle on Fridays, and the website booked nothing after 5 pm. People were finding the practice. They were not becoming appointments.

So before anything else, do this test tonight: call your own number at 5:30 pm, and tap your own booking link on your phone. If the call goes to voicemail and the link opens a contact form that someone reads on Monday, every other idea on this page sends more people to the same closed door.

What fixed it in Dallas was unglamorous: a text back on every missed call, appointment reminders, and a reply to every inquiry within minutes, day or night. That is the whole of our [patient follow-up service](/services/) in one sentence.

## 3. Give your best treatment its own page and its own campaign

A general "new patients welcome" message competes with every dentist in town. A page about one treatment, written for the person who is deciding, competes with far fewer.

Pick the treatment you want more of and can deliver more of. For many practices that is implants, clear aligners or cosmetic work. Then give it its own page on your website, with the three things that patient wants to know before they come in: what it involves, roughly what it costs or how financing works, and what happens at the first visit.

In Dallas that was one implant funnel with three screening questions. The questions do two jobs. They answer the financing question up front, which is where most implant leads go quiet, and they tell the front desk which people are ready now, so those are the ones who get called first.

## 4. Keep your Google Business Profile honest and current

For a local practice, the profile is what most new patients see before your website. The work is smaller than people think: the right primary category, real opening hours, one phone number that a person answers, and a booking link that leads to a page where someone can actually ask for a time.

Then ten minutes a month: confirm the hours, answer the reviews, add two or three real photos, and look at the profile on your own phone. We wrote the full checklist in [Google Business Profile for dentists: what to fix first](/blog/google-business-profile-for-dentists/).

## 5. Ask for reviews after every good visit, and answer them safely

Reviews do more for a dental practice than any other single thing on Google. Ask at the moment the patient is happiest, usually right after a visit that went well, and make it one tap. A steady few each week looks natural and is worth more than a burst of twenty.

Answer every review within a few days. Thank the person, keep it short, and never confirm they are your patient or mention their treatment, even if they wrote about it themselves. Patient privacy rules apply to your reply. For a complaint, invite them to call the office so it can be handled privately.

## 6. Advertise one treatment at a time, in your own accounts

Ads are further down this list on purpose. They work, but only once the first five ideas are in place, because an ad sends people to the phone, the booking link and the treatment page you just fixed.

Google and Facebook do different jobs. Google reaches people who are already searching, for example "dentist near me" or "dental implants near me". Facebook and Instagram reach people who are not searching yet, which suits elective treatments such as aligners and cosmetic work. Give each treatment its own campaign, and judge each one by booked patients, not clicks.

Two rules we keep. The ad accounts stay in the practice's name, so the history and the data are yours if you ever change agency. And nothing goes live until the dentist has approved it, because your state dental board has rules on what an ad may say, and they vary from state to state.

## 7. Look at four numbers every Monday

Most dental marketing fails quietly, because nobody is counting the right thing. Clicks go up, the report looks good, and Friday is still empty.

Count four things, every week, on one page: inquiries, booked appointments, patients who showed, and the revenue they brought. If an idea on this list moves those numbers, keep it. If it does not, stop it, however good it looks on a chart.

That is the report we send every Monday. In the Dallas owner's words: "The weekly report is the only marketing email I actually open."

## What Dallas looked like after six months

Over six months, that practice booked 184 new patients, and 167 of them showed up. In the last month measured, $48k of treatment was attributed to the work. They are hiring a third hygienist.

None of that came from one clever idea. It came from the order: the follow-up first, then the recall, then one treatment page and campaign, then the reviews, with the numbers checked every week. The full case is on our [results page](/results/), and the way we do it for dental practices is on our [dental marketing page](/industries/dental/).

## Ideas you can skip

Discount specials and free exams. Several states require the ad to list every charge that can follow, and for Medicare and Medicaid patients a free service can count as an improper inducement. We do not run them.

Posting on social media every day. A few real photos a month on your profile and your site do the job. Daily posting is a lot of work that rarely books anyone.

Buying lists of "leads". The people on them did not ask for a dentist. Your own past patients did, once.

## Where to start this week

1. Tonight, call your own number at 5:30 pm and tap your own booking link.
1. This week, pull the list of patients not seen in a year or more and write to them in the dentist's voice.
1. Next week, pick the one treatment you want more of and give it a page of its own.

Everything else on this list builds on those three.

